WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.760
Now, what year was this?

2
00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.540
This was 1934.

3
00:00:03.540 --> 00:00:14.940
I graduated in ’36, and a master’s degree in linguistics and broadcasting in ’38.

4
00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:29.280
I was influenced a great deal by Dr. Harley Smith, who was on the speech faculty and was a superb teacher and good person.

5
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.100
One of the things that impressed me about his teaching is that he would ask,

6
00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:41.380
“Before you do anything, it would be useful if you would ask yourself, ‘What’s the purpose of this?’

7
00:00:41.380 --> 00:00:46.720
Whether it’s writing or whether it’s acting.

8
00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:53.280
If you start with a sense of what the purpose of this is, it would be a lot easier to do.”

9
00:00:53.280 --> 00:01:02.660
And so part of my life has been marked by an internal sense of questioning, what is the purpose of this before I start.

10
00:01:02.660 --> 00:01:15.500
So I owe that to Dr. Harley Smith, who through his efforts, I became the first Director of Broadcasting at the university, and the first,

11
00:01:15.500 --> 00:01:25.320
I guess . . . He taught radio classes and I first became his assistant and replaced him as he went into other fields.

12
00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:28.600
What was the locus of speech and broadcasting?

13
00:01:28.600 --> 00:01:30.140
In what building?

14
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:32.380
Music and Dramatic Arts building.

15
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:33.820
The half to the right as you entered.

16
00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.460
Yes.

17
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:41.820
And because I knew quite a few of the people and faculty in Music because of my broadcast activities,

18
00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:49.540
of course, I always narrated yearly broadcasts of The Messiah.

19
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.200
Jo sang.

20
00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.540
I never met her.

21
00:01:52.540 --> 00:02:00.940
So my future wife and I were in the same building for some years and never met.

22
00:02:00.940 --> 00:02:14.540
But I was very fortunate to receive a Rockefeller Foundation Fellowship that occurred as my master’s year ended.

23
00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:22.680
I suddenly had to complete my thesis in six weeks instead of three months.

24
00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:31.360
So I worked day and night and finished my thesis to get on the train for New York and the Rockefeller Foundation.

25
00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.840
Never have caught up that sleep.

26
00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.080
I thought I would do it in New York, but I never did.
